## Why This Is Asked

This question explores a critical real-world scenario: testing serverless, event-driven processing with strict data integrity guarantees and partial-failure resilience. Candidates must demonstrate understanding of idempotency patterns, event-driven architectures, and comprehensive testing strategies.

## Key Concepts

- **Idempotency**: Ensuring duplicate S3 events for the same file produce the same result without duplication in DynamoDB
- **S3/Lambda Integration**: Understanding event-driven triggers and their testing challenges
- **Chunked Processing**: Handling large files efficiently without memory exhaustion
- **Partial Failure Resilience**: Maintaining accurate counts and data consistency when failures occur mid-processing
- **CI/CD Visibility**: Using mocks, stubs, and integration tests to verify correctness in automated pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Idempotent guard: check if file was already processed
async function handleEvent(event, db, s3) {
  const bucket = event.bucket;
  const key = event.key;

  const readStream = await s3.getObject(bucket, key).createReadStream();
  const checksum = await computeChecksum(readStream);

  const existing = await db.get(`FILE#${checksum}`);
  if (existing) {
    return { statusCode: 200, body: 'Already processed' };
  }

  await processChunks(readStream, db, checksum);
  await db.put(`FILE#${checksum}`, { processedAt: Date.now() });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests resilience and reliability fundamentals using real-world API interaction patterns. It evaluates the ability to design concrete tests, leverage mocks effectively, and reason about backoff strategies, idempotency, and rate limits—core skills for building dependable services.

## Key Concepts

- API mocking with deterministic fixtures
- Retry and backoff strategies with configurable caps
- Idempotent retry semantics to ensure safety
- Per-client rate limiting under concurrent scenarios
- CI integration for reproducible, automated testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example scaffold using a mocking library
const nock = require('nock');
const { pricingClient } = require('./pricingClient');

describe('Pricing API Client', () => {
  beforeEach(() => nock.cleanAll());

  test('retries on 429 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(429, 'Too Many Requests', { 'Retry-After': '1' })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 99.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(99.99);
    expect(scope.isDone()).toBe(true);
  });

  test('retries on 500 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(500)
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 49.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(49.99);
  });

  test('respects max retry cap', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .times(4)
      .reply(500);

    await expect(pricingClient.getPrice()).rejects.toThrow('Max retries exceeded');
  });

  test('idempotent retries do not cause side effects', async () => {
    let callCount = 0;
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(() => {
        callCount++;
        return [500, {}];
      })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 100 });

    await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(callCount).toBe(1); // First call failed, second succeeded
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates testing approaches for real-time ML moderation systems with canary deployments, privacy requirements, and drift detection capabilities—extending beyond basic functional testing.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency and throughput optimization
- Data privacy and PII masking protocols
- Canary deployment strategies with automated rollback
- Model drift detection and monitoring systems
- Contract testing between microservices and components

## Code Example
```javascript
// Latency contract validation (pseudo-code)
async function validateLatencyContract(moderationRequest) {
  const startTime = Date.now();
  const result = await moderationService.process(moderationRequest);
  const latency = Date.now() - startTime;

  if (latency > 200) {
    throw new Error(`SLA breach: ${latency}ms > 200ms`);
  }

  return { result, latency };
}

// Drift detection monitoring
function detectModelDrift(currentMetrics, baselineMetrics) {
  const precisionDrift = Math.abs(currentMetrics.precision - baselineMetrics.precision);
  const recallDrift = Math.abs(currentMetrics.recall - baselineMetrics.recall);

  return precisionDrift > 0.05 || recallDrift > 0.05;
}
```

